  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Apple Canker is a fungal disease caused by Neonectria ditissima that affects apple and other woody plants in New England, characterized by sunken lesions on branches and trunks. The disease can girdle branches, causing dieback and significant economic losses in orchards if left untreated.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Canker appearance",
      "description": "Look for roughly circular, sunken lesions with concentric rings of callus tissue on branches and trunks, often with a characteristic target-like pattern",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Fruiting bodies",
      "description": "Small red or orange perithecia (flask-shaped structures) may be visible on canker surfaces, especially in spring and fall",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Branch damage",
      "description": "Infected branches may exhibit branch dieback, wilting, or complete girdling that kills tissue beyond the canker",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Apple Canker thrives in cool, moist climates of New England and prefers stressed or injured trees, spreading through pruning wounds and winter damage.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The fungus has two sexual stages in its life cycle and can persist in cankers for years, releasing spores during wet spring and fall weather to infect new host tissues.",
